r yesterday with his uh best friend  his best friend was rescued uh this 14  year old child lost his life we want no  more loss of life but also we need the  london fire brigade to be assisted by us  by doing the right thing  so just on that swimming i mean tragic  as these terrible incidents are mr mayor  even if you've been wild swimming as the  as the phrase now is you know  the stretch of water  you know exactly what the conditions are  just don't do it for the next few days  can i come at this point look accidents  happen not deliberately right people get  into trouble when they're swimming not  intentionally  and the point is at this stage i can't  hand in heart say there'll be a fire  fighter who'll be able to jump in and  save you or a lifeguard to be able to  jump in and save you if you're getting  to bother  and you know these tragedies we've had  over the last few days a 14 year old  child yesterday a 16 year old child two  three days ago neither of them intended  to have serious injuries themselves or  losing their lives and so i am asking  people  not to be tempted to have fun buy a  family barbecue in their back garden  tonight or by jumping into water uh  tonight the london fire brigade do not  call a major incident lightly the number  of incidents they're responding to is  almost five-fold what they normally have  to deal with  we've  asked the fire service to council leave  their council leave they brought in  staff those who were doing other things  have stopped doing other things all the  engines that can be out are being out  but there's the difference between a six  minute response and a 20-minute response  is the difference potentially between  life and death and all your personal  possessions being lost so i am saying uh  no barbecues tonight on the basis of the  advice i am saying don't jump into the  river thames or canals or waterways  is really really important we do what we  can to get through this heat wave  without unnecessary deaths wi